---
// /loaners — the standing invitation to headphone manufacturers.
//
// WHO THIS PAGE IS FOR, because it changes every word on it: not a customer. A product manager or a
// marketing coordinator who has been handed a request and needs to decide, usually without talking to
// anyone. So it answers the whole decision in one screen — what we need, what it costs them, what
// comes back, what we will and won't publish — and it is written to survive being forwarded.
//
// It deliberately does NOT lead with Attune, and never mentions voice-match. "Make your $200
// headphone sound like the $2,000 one" is a fine consumer pitch and a hostile one to the company
// selling the $2,000 headphone. See warren-labs/docs/loaner-program.md §5.
//
// It also never claims we sell no gear (moat-strategy.md Pillar 4 — a retail store is still live).
// Every claim here is about the DATA: we publish what the rig says.
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import Button from '../components/Button.astro';

const SITE = 'https://warrenlabs.com';
const description =
  'Warren Labs measures headphones on a documented bench and returns the raw data to the manufacturer. ' +
  'Loan us a unit for two weeks: you cover shipping out, we insure and pay the return, and you get the ' +
  'measurement file for your own use.';

const TERMS = [
  ['What we need', 'One retail unit, on loan, for two weeks.'],
  ['What it costs you', 'Shipping out. We insure and pay the return.'],
  ['How it comes back', 'In its box, unmodified, nothing disassembled, firmware untouched.'],
  ['What you get', 'The published page — and the raw measurement file, for your own use, no conditions.'],
  ['Turnaround', 'Measured within a week of arrival. The page goes up when it is.'],
  ['Multiple pad sets', 'We measure each and publish the difference.'],
];

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: `${SITE}/` },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'For manufacturers', item: `${SITE}/loaners` },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Layout title="For manufacturers — loan us a headphone · Warren Labs" description={description} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <Nav variant="home" />

  <main class="wrap ln">
    <nav class="crumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><span aria-current="page">For manufacturers</span>
    </nav>

    <span class="eyebrow">For manufacturers</span>
    <h1>Send us the headphone.<br />We&rsquo;ll send you back the data.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Warren Labs is a measurement bench in Livingston, Montana. Loan us a unit for two weeks and we
      measure it on a documented rig, publish the result, and <b>give you the raw measurement file for
      your own use</b> &mdash; free, and with no conditions attached to it.
    </p>

    <div class="terms">
      {TERMS.map(([k, v]) => (
        <div class="term">
          <span class="tk">{k}</span>
          <span class="tv" set:html={v} />
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>

    <div class="cta-row">
      <Button href="mailto:jamey@warrenlabs.com?subject=Headphone%20loan" variant="primary">Email us about a loan</Button>
      <Button href="/graphs" variant="ghost">See a measurement first</Button>
    </div>

    <section class="sec">
      <h2>What we will and won&rsquo;t publish</h2>
      <p>
        The honest part first, because it is the whole point: <b>we publish what the rig says,
        flattering or not.</b> If that is not a deal you want, this is the right moment to say no, and
        no hard feelings &mdash; a measurement that came with conditions would not be worth anything to
        your customers or to us.
      </p>
      <p>
        What we will publish is a frequency response, the fixture it was measured on, the seating
        procedure, how repeatable that seating was, and where the measurement stops being trustworthy.
        <b>What we won&rsquo;t publish is a rank, a score, or a subjective verdict.</b> We are not a
        review outlet and we don&rsquo;t audition products against each other for sport.
      </p>
      <p>
        We also say the obvious thing on every page, before anyone else has to: <b>one unit is one
        unit, not the model.</b> A single measurement of a single sample is exactly that, and pretending
        otherwise is how measurement gets a bad name.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="sec">
      <h2>You can read the method before you decide</h2>
      <p>
        This is the part we think is genuinely different. Our measurement method is written down and
        published <em>before</em> anything gets measured, so you can read exactly how your headphone
        will be treated and decide with the facts in front of you rather than hoping.
      </p>
      <p>
        The short version: we seat the headphone <b>deliberately, for the best fit</b> &mdash; adjusted
        for seal and for sound, the way a listener adjusts a headphone before settling in &mdash; rather
        than dropping it on at random. The common convention averages several careless placements
        together; we think that answers the wrong question, because nobody listens at a random seating.
        What we publish is <b>one real seating, never a blend</b>, chosen by a stated rule, with the
        repeatability reported alongside it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Both channels are measured and kept. Nothing is smoothed into looking better than it is.
      </p>
      <div class="cta-row tight">
        <Button href="/learn/why-two-rigs-cant-share-an-axis" variant="ghost">How we think about rigs</Button>
        <Button href="/learn/how-to-read-a-frequency-response-graph" variant="ghost">How to read the graph</Button>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="sec">
      <h2>If your headphone ships with more than one pad set</h2>
      <p>
        Then that is the interesting measurement, and almost nobody publishes it properly. Pads move a
        response more than most people expect, and comparisons across the internet are usually made
        between different rigs, different units, and different seatings all at once &mdash; which tells
        you nothing about the pads.
      </p>
      <p>
        Send the pad sets and we measure each one on the same rig, the same unit, the same seating
        procedure, and publish the difference. If you have a pad revision you want checked against the
        outgoing one, that works too.
      </p>
    </section>

    {/* The loan program is one unit, and the page has already said out loud that one unit is not
        the model. That admission is the natural opening for the paid service, so it goes here
        rather than in the CTA. Deep-links to the block on /design-review until it has its own
        page. Deliberately absent, here as there: the protocol, the fixture, and the analysis. */}
    <section class="sec">
      <h2>If you need the run, not one unit</h2>
      <p>
        Everything above is one unit, and one unit is not the model. When the question is the
        <b>run</b> instead, meaning how much your units vary from each other, how well the channels
        match on each one, and which ones fall outside your own tolerance, that is a paid service
        rather than a loan.
      </p>
      <p>
        Send a sample from your production units and we measure every one on the same rig under one
        documented seating protocol, then report unit-to-unit and channel-to-channel variance
        against your design intent. You get the report, the graphs, and the raw data.
        <a href="/design-review/#production-measurement">From $750, depending on sample size.</a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="sec">
      <h2>Who runs this</h2>
      <p>
        Jamey Warren. I was employee #1 at Grace Design, where one of my jobs was building the
        automated measurement system the gear was tested against, and I later ran HeadRoom as
        president and CEO. I do the measuring myself, on my own bench, and the method above is the one
        I actually follow.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="ln-cta">
      <div>
        <h2 class="cta-h">Happy to answer anything first.</h2>
        <p>
          Everything on this page is written to be forwarded internally. If it&rsquo;s easier to talk it
          through, that works too.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="cta-btns">
        <Button href="mailto:jamey@warrenlabs.com?subject=Headphone%20loan" variant="primary">Get in touch</Button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>

  <Footer variant="home" />
</Layout>

<style>
  .ln { padding: 56px 24px 24px; }
  .crumb {
    font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 11.5px; color: var(--muted-2);
    display: flex; gap: 9px; margin-bottom: 26px;
  }
  .crumb a:hover { color: var(--text); }
  .crumb [aria-current] { color: var(--muted); }
  .ln h1 {
    font-family: var(--serif); font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(34px, 5.2vw, 54px); line-height: 1.05; margin: 12px 0 0;
  }
  .lede {
    color: var(--muted); font-size: 18px; line-height: 1.6;
    max-width: 64ch; margin: 20px 0 0; text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  /* The terms table is the load-bearing element: it answers the whole decision without a call. */
  .terms {
    margin-top: 40px; border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--surface); overflow: hidden;
  }
  .term {
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: 220px 1fr; gap: 20px;
    padding: 16px 22px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); align-items: baseline;
  }
  .term:last-child { border-bottom: 0; }
  .tk {
    font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 10.5px; letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--accent-soft);
  }
  .tv { font-size: 15.5px; line-height: 1.55; color: var(--text); }
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .term { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 6px; }
  }

  .cta-row { display: flex; gap: 12px; flex-wrap: wrap; margin-top: 32px; }
  .cta-row.tight { margin-top: 20px; }

  .sec { margin-top: 52px; max-width: 68ch; }
  .sec h2 {
    font-family: var(--serif); font-weight: 400; font-size: 26px; margin: 0 0 14px;
  }
  .sec p {
    color: var(--muted); font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 14px; text-wrap: pretty;
  }
  .sec p b { color: var(--text); font-weight: 500; }

  .ln-cta {
    margin-top: 60px;
    background: linear-gradient(120deg, rgba(255, 158, 44, 0.07), var(--surface) 55%);
    border: 1px solid var(--accent-line); border-radius: 18px; padding: 30px 32px;
    display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 24px; flex-wrap: wrap;
  }
  .cta-h {
    font-family: var(--serif); font-weight: 400; font-size: 24px; margin: 0; max-width: 24ch;
  }
  .ln-cta p { color: var(--muted); font-size: 15px; margin: 8px 0 0; max-width: 52ch; }
  .cta-btns { display: flex; gap: 12px; flex-wrap: wrap; }
</style>
